    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "74",
      "title": "Natural Immunity vs. Vaccines: What 1,600+ Pregnant Women Think",
      "description": "",
      "date": "May 29, 2025",
      "read_time": "2 min read",
      "author": "Dianah Otieno",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "74-Banner.jpg"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Navigating personal health decisions is a journey we all undertake, often with significant considerations for our well-being and those around us. For pregnant individuals, these choices carry a unique weight as they strive to protect their own health while also safeguarding their developing child from potential risks, such as infectious diseases. But what drives their decisions, especially when it comes to vaccinations?"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The Centre of Excellence in Women and Child Health, East Africa (CoEWCH, EA) led a groundbreaking WHO-led research study, conducted by Aga Khan University (AKU) researchers and the World Health Organisation-Human Reproduction Programme Alliance (WHO-HRP), in Brazil, Ghana, Kenya, and Pakistan. This cross-sectional study investigates pregnant women's attitudes and intentions towards vaccines in pregnancy, including COVID-19 vaccination (VAXGEN project) and how the decision-making occurs among pregnant and postpartum women. The study sought to inform demand generation and communication strategies that address women's fears, experiences, challenges, and opportunities throughout pregnancy and the post-pregnancy period."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "To conclude this research study, AKU organised a two-day capacity-building and dissemination workshop with the African Population and Health Research Center (APHRC) and the WHO-HRP Alliance Hub. The capacity-building component of the workshop targeted early career researchers from AKU and APHRC, providing training in developing policy briefs for communicating research findings to policymakers."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The research team disseminated the findings of the VAXGEN study to involved parties, including maternal and child health researchers, health professionals, public health officials, science communication journalists, and non-government organisation (NGO) officials. The findings are both eye-opening and hopeful. Among 1,603 pregnant women surveyed, the top concerns were vaccine safety for their baby and the belief that natural immunity is better. The top reasons for vaccination were the protection of the baby and themselves. The top reasons against vaccination are safety concerns, fear of harming the baby, and doubts about vaccine effectiveness."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "These insights are critical as new vaccines for adults, including pregnant women, enter the pipeline. Understanding what drives vaccine acceptance-or hesitation-can help shape clinical trials and public health strategies to ensure these life-saving tools reach those who need them."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For pregnant women, the decision to vaccinate is deeply personal, rooted in love and a fierce desire to protect",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". As science advances, so must CoEWCH, EA's efforts to address concerns, build trust, and empower women to make informed choices for themselves and their babies. After all, when it comes to maternal and neonatal health, every shot counts."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "For more information on this study, visit: "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "https://www.aku.edu/coe-wch",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "link",
                        "attrs": {
                          "href": "/coe-wch",
